<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Julien Raimond</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1744">1744</a>–<a href="/years/1801">1801</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Julien Raimond was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a> planter of the French colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a>, a free man of colour who became a leader in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution">Haitian Revolution</a> and the formation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>. A slaveholder with over 100 slaves by the 1780s, he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1785 to campaign against the colony's racially discriminatory laws, and from 1789 pressed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a>, working with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vincent-oge">Vincent Ogé</a>, Henri Grégoire and the Society of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Friends</a> of the Blacks. On 15 May 1791 the legislature gave wealthy free-born men of colour the vote, and white colonists' resistance led to civil war. Raimond later returned to Saint-Domingue, helped write the 1801 Constitution under Toussaint L'Ouverture and died soon after.</p></div>
